## Your strongest conversion asset is probably hidden

When we audit websites at Myntmore, we frequently see founders hide their strongest conversion assets: real-world outcomes, case studies, and client logos. They bury them below the fold or, worse, leave them undocumented in messy Slack threads and random scratchpads.

> “People believe other people more than your headline.” (Tejas Jhaveri, Founder of Myntmore)

In a noisy B2B environment, prospects are constantly asking two questions: “Do I trust them?” and “Is this actually for me?” Concrete, data-backed proof answers both questions faster than another polished claim ever could.

## Why strong wins never become case studies

The problem is rarely a lack of results. Starting from a blank page is tedious, so the write-up gets pushed to next week. The details grow fuzzy, the customer moves on to a new priority, and the momentum dies.

When teams finally draft the story, they often overcorrect with jargon. A simple customer journey becomes abstract fluff about “transforming digital ecosystems globally.” The result sounds impressive but proves very little.

## Write your wins before they cool off

Document the problem, the work, and the measurable result while the project is still fresh. Keep the customer at the center of the story, use the exact language they use, and make every number traceable to a real source.

## Capture proof while the work is happening

The easiest case study to write is the one you started collecting on day one. Keep a short running note for every engagement, and add to it whenever something changes:

- **The starting point:** what the customer was trying to fix, in their words, and the numbers that described the problem at the time.
- **The decision:** why they chose to act now, and what they had tried before.
- **The work:** the main things your team did, and anything you changed along the way.
- **The result:** what moved, by how much and over what period, with the source of each number.
- **The quote:** one or two sentences from the customer, written down the moment they say something specific.

Five minutes a week on this note saves hours of reconstruction later, and it keeps the numbers honest because they were recorded when they happened.

## A simple structure: situation, approach, result

Most strong B2B case studies follow the same three-part shape.

1. **Situation.** Who the customer is, described by type and size if you cannot name them, and the problem they faced. Specific beats impressive: "a 40-person logistics software company with no outbound system" tells a reader more than "a fast-growing technology leader."
2. **Approach.** What you did, in the order you did it. Include the choices you made and why, since that is what a prospect is really evaluating.
3. **Result.** The measurable outcome and the time it took. One clear number with context is more credible than a list of percentages with none.

Keep it short. A page a prospect can read in two minutes gets read; a ten-page document gets saved for later and forgotten.

## Get sign-off, or anonymize properly

Many wins stay undocumented because nobody wants to ask the customer for permission. Ask early, ideally when the result first lands and the customer is pleased. Offer them a draft to review, and let them change anything that feels sensitive.

If they would rather not be named, you can still publish. Describe them by industry, size and market instead of by name, and check that the combination of details does not identify them anyway. Keep every number exactly as it happened. An anonymous case study with real figures is still strong proof; a named one with inflated figures is a liability.

## Where to use case studies in outbound

A case study does most of its work in places other than your case studies page:

- **In the first email or LinkedIn message:** one line that names a similar customer type and the result, such as the number of meetings booked for a company like theirs.
- **In follow-ups:** a short link to the full story, sent to prospects who showed interest but did not reply.
- **On the pages prospects check:** your homepage, service pages and industry pages, placed near the call to action rather than below the fold.
- **In sales conversations:** sent after a discovery call, matched to the problem the prospect described.

The best match beats the best result. A prospect in pharma cares more about a modest result for a pharma company than an impressive one for a software firm, so build a small library that covers the industries and problems you sell into. You can see how we organize ours on the [case studies page](/case-studies).
